  content: `
<h2>Why good students still lose marks in Literature</h2>
<p>Many students who love reading still get disappointing grades in Literature in English. The reason is rarely that they did not understand the book. It is that their answers <em>retell</em> instead of <em>argue</em>. They write: "First Okonkwo was a strong man, then he went to exile, then his village changed." That is a summary. The examiner is looking for something different: a clear answer to the exact question, supported by evidence from the text, with an explanation of why that evidence matters.</p>
<p>The good news is that this is a skill, and skills can be practised. You do not need fancy vocabulary. You need a structure you can use on any question, under time pressure, without panic.</p>
<p>A word on specifics. The format of the papers, the number of questions you must answer, the marks and the list of prescribed texts are set by WAEC and change from time to time. Always check the current WAEC Literature in English syllabus and the current list of set books. This post teaches the part that does not change: how to build an answer.</p>
[[callout key|What the examiner is really asking|Every essay question is a small argument. Your job is to take a position in your first lines, prove it with evidence from the text, explain how the evidence supports your position, and finish by returning to the question.]]

<h2>Read the question like an examiner</h2>
<p>Marks are lost before a single sentence is written, because students answer the question they hoped for instead of the question that was asked. Spend the first two minutes on the question itself.</p>
<h3>Find the command word</h3>
<p>Command words tell you what to do. They usually look like these.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Discuss:</strong> look at more than one side or aspect, and reach a judgement.</li>
<li><strong>Examine / Analyse:</strong> break the idea into parts and show how each works.</li>
<li><strong>Explain:</strong> say what it means and why it happens.</li>
<li><strong>Illustrate / With close reference to the text:</strong> your evidence is the main point.</li>
<li><strong>To what extent:</strong> take a position on how far the statement is true. Do not simply say yes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Find the topic and the limit</h3>
<p>Underline the subject (a theme, a character, a technique) and the limit (a particular act, chapter, a poem, a relationship). A question about the use of symbolism in a poem must be answered with symbols, not with a general theme summary. A question on "the role of women" must discuss women, not men who happen to appear beside them.</p>
[[flow Read the question twice ; Underline command word ; Underline topic and limit ; Write a one-line answer ; List three pieces of evidence ; Write]]
<p>That one-line answer, sometimes called a thesis, is the most useful sentence you will write. It stops you drifting into plot summary because every paragraph must support it.</p>

<h2>The PEEL paragraph: your basic building block</h2>
<p>Almost every body paragraph of a good literature answer follows the same four-part pattern.</p>
[[steps Point::Begin with a sentence that answers part of the question directly. ; Evidence::Give a quotation, an event, an image or a stage action from the text. ; Explanation::Say what the evidence shows and why it supports your point. ; Link::Tie the paragraph back to the question in one sentence.]]
<p>Here it is in action on a made-up but realistic question about a prose text. The example uses the well-known novel Things Fall Apart by Chinua Achebe, which has appeared on school reading lists. Check which texts are currently prescribed for your examination.</p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Discuss how fear shapes Okonkwo's actions.</p>
<p><strong>Weak paragraph:</strong> "Okonkwo is a strong man who beats his wives and later kills Ikemefuna. He was also sent into exile after an accident at a funeral. Later he returned and things had changed."</p>
<p><strong>PEEL paragraph:</strong> "Fear of being seen as weak drives Okonkwo's worst decisions. When the elders agree that Ikemefuna must be killed, Okonkwo joins the men even though the boy calls him father, because he dreads being thought soft. The narrator tells us his whole life is ruled by fear of failure and weakness, so his violence is less courage than a desperate defence against shame. This shows that Okonkwo's strength is built on fear, which is the central irony of his character."</p>
<p>The second paragraph is not longer by much, but it is doing four jobs. It answers the question, uses a precise event, explains the meaning and links back to the idea of fear. That is the difference between a pass and a distinction.</p>

<h2>Introductions and conclusions that do not waste time</h2>
<p>Students often write long, empty introductions such as "Literature is the mirror of society and since the beginning of time writers have used it to teach lessons". Examiners have read that sentence thousands of times. It earns nothing.</p>
<h3>A three-sentence introduction</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Context:</strong> name the text and the writer, and the part of the book your question concerns.</li>
<li><strong>Position:</strong> a direct answer to the question, in your own words.</li>
<li><strong>Road map:</strong> the points you will use, in one clause, so the examiner knows where you are going.</li>
</ol>
<p>For the question on fear: "In Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe presents Okonkwo as a man whose actions are driven by fear. His fear of weakness leads him to violence against his family, to the killing of Ikemefuna and to his tragic end. These episodes show how pride built on fear destroys the man who guards it."</p>
<h3>A two-sentence conclusion</h3>
<p>Do not introduce new points. Briefly restate your position in fresh words and say what it shows about the text as a whole. "Okonkwo's fear is therefore the engine of the novel's tragedy. Achebe uses it to show how rigid ideas of manhood can destroy both the individual and his world." Done.</p>
[[callout warn|Avoid these opening lines|"Since the beginning of time", "The dictionary defines literature as", and "In this essay I will" add words without adding marks. Start with the text and your position.]]

<h2>Using evidence: quotations, events and details</h2>
<p>Evidence is the engine of a literature essay. Without it, your answer is an opinion. With it, it becomes an argument. There are three types of evidence and you should use all three.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Direct quotation:</strong> a short phrase placed inside your own sentence. Keep it brief, because you only need to prove a point, not copy a page.</li>
<li><strong>Precise event:</strong> a scene or episode named clearly: who, where, what happened.</li>
<li><strong>Technique:</strong> a feature the writer used, such as irony, repetition, flashback, symbolism, soliloquy, imagery or proverbs.</li>
</ul>
<h3>How to learn quotations without memorising a whole book</h3>
<p>Pick six to eight short quotations for each major character or theme. A line of up to ten words is easier to remember than a paragraph. Write them on small cards: the quotation on one side, who says it, where in the text and what it shows on the other. Test yourself with the cards for a few minutes each day. If you cannot recall exact words, a reliable paraphrase is better than a wrong quotation.</p>
<p>Never invent quotations. An invented line, when the examiner knows the book, costs credibility. If you remember the sense but not the wording, write "the narrator suggests that" and describe it accurately.</p>

<h2>Drama, prose and poetry: what changes in each</h2>
<p>The PEEL paragraph works across all three genres. What changes is the kind of evidence and the technical vocabulary.</p>
<h3>Drama</h3>
<p>Plays are written to be performed. Show that you know this. Refer to dialogue, stage directions, entrances and exits, soliloquies and asides, and the way scenes are arranged. When you discuss a character, ask what the audience sees and hears, not only what the character says. Use the word "audience" rather than "reader" where it fits.</p>
<h3>Prose</h3>
<p>For novels and short stories, think about plot, setting, characterisation, narrator and point of view, style and theme. Discuss how the author tells the story as well as what happens. Mention the narrator's voice, the use of flashback, the proverbs in Achebe or the use of dialogue to reveal character.</p>
<h3>Poetry</h3>
<p>Poetry questions often ask about theme, tone, imagery or form. Quote short phrases and then analyse them. The word choice (diction), images, sound devices such as alliteration, rhythm, structure and the way a poem moves from stanza to stanza are all fair evidence. A strong poetry paragraph names a device, quotes it and explains the effect on the reader.</p>
[[compare Weak poetry answer|Strong poetry answer|The poet uses nice words ; The poem is about sadness ; Alliteration is used ; Lists every device without comment|The poet uses the image of a fading light to suggest loss ; The sad tone is built through slow, heavy rhythm ; Alliteration of the s sound creates a hushed, whispering mood ; Chooses two or three devices and explains the effect of each]]
<p>A useful sentence frame for poetry is: "The poet uses (device) in the line (quotation) to show (meaning), which makes the reader feel (effect)." Use it as a prompt, not as a script to repeat in every paragraph.</p>

<h2>A fully worked answer, start to finish</h2>
<p>Let us put everything together in a realistic planning exercise. You have about thirty to thirty-five minutes for an essay question, depending on how many you must answer. Check the real time and question count in the current syllabus and past papers.</p>
[[qa Plan an answer to: Discuss the theme of power in a drama text you have studied. Show how the playwright develops it.|Read the question. Command word: discuss. Topic: power. Limit: how the playwright develops it, so technique matters too. ; Write the one-line answer: the play shows that power can corrupt those who hold it and is challenged by those who suffer under it. ; Choose three points: how power is gained, how it is abused, how it is resisted or ends. ; For each point, choose one scene and one short quotation or a precise action on stage. ; Add one technique per point, such as dramatic irony, a soliloquy or symbolic props. ; Draft a three-sentence introduction naming the text, the position and the three points. ; Write three PEEL paragraphs with a link back to the question at the end of each. ; Close with two sentences returning to the position and stating what it shows about the play as a whole.|A plan of about five lines, written in two or three minutes, becomes a structured answer of an introduction, three body paragraphs and a short conclusion, with each paragraph holding a point, evidence, explanation and link.]]
<p>Notice that the plan is built before writing begins. Students who skip the plan tend to write in the order they remember things, which is usually the order of the plot, and that produces a summary.</p>

<h2>Managing your time in the examination hall</h2>
<p>Literature is a writing paper, and running out of time is a common problem, especially for students who write a long first answer and rush the rest. Divide your time before you start.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What to do</th><th>Roughly how long</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Choose</td><td>Read the questions and pick the ones you can support with evidence</td><td>A few minutes at the start</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plan</td><td>Write a five-line plan for each essay before writing it</td><td>2 to 3 minutes each</td></tr>
<tr><td>Write</td><td>Introduction, body paragraphs, conclusion</td><td>Most of the time for each question</td></tr>
<tr><td>Check</td><td>Read for missing words, names and sentences that do not finish</td><td>A minute or two each</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Give each question equal time if the marks are equal, and stop when your time is up so that the last question is not starved. An incomplete third essay costs far more than a slightly shorter first one.</p>
<h3>Choosing questions wisely</h3>
<p>Choose questions where you can name at least three specific scenes or moments. Avoid questions that only sound familiar. If a question asks about a minor character you barely remember, leave it. Choose according to your evidence, not according to the title that looks easiest.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes and how to fix them</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Telling the story:</strong> every paragraph should start with a point, not with "then" or "after that".</li>
<li><strong>No evidence:</strong> statements such as "the writer is a good storyteller" need an example from the text.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence without explanation:</strong> a quotation that sits alone does not earn marks. Always say what it shows.</li>
<li><strong>Answering a different question:</strong> a question on the role of women is not a chance to write about the hero.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring technique:</strong> where the question asks how the writer presents something, you must discuss methods, not only content.</li>
<li><strong>Messy handwriting and spelling of names:</strong> spell character and author names correctly, copying them from the question paper if needed.</li>
<li><strong>Using notes from another school's guide without understanding:</strong> examiners reward your own reading, not memorised model essays.</li>
</ul>
[[bars Where answers usually lose marks|Retelling the plot=35 ; Thin evidence=30 ; No explanation=20 ; Ignoring the question=15|% (illustration only)]]
<p>The chart above is an illustration of common patterns, not statistics. Use it as a prompt to check your own scripts for these four habits. If you have a teacher or a friend, swap essays and tick each of these as you read.</p>

<h2>Writing style: clear, formal and in the present tense</h2>
<p>You do not need a heavy vocabulary to score well, but a few style habits make your answers read as confident and controlled.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Use the present tense for the text.</strong> Write "Okonkwo kills Ikemefuna" rather than "Okonkwo killed Ikemefuna". Literary events are treated as always happening on the page.</li>
<li><strong>Keep sentences short and clear.</strong> One idea per sentence beats a long, tangled one that loses its subject.</li>
<li><strong>Use the writer's name correctly.</strong> Say "Achebe presents" or "the playwright shows", not "the book tells us".</li>
<li><strong>Avoid slang and text-message spelling.</strong> Keep a formal tone throughout.</li>
<li><strong>Use linking words with purpose.</strong> Words such as however, therefore, in contrast and as a result show the examiner how your ideas connect.</li>
</ul>
<p>Read your practice essays aloud. If you stumble over a sentence, it is probably too long or badly built.</p>

<h2>How to practise in the last weeks before the exam</h2>
<p>You will not improve by only rereading the books. You improve by writing and getting feedback. Here is a sensible practice routine.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Build a text summary sheet:</strong> for each book, list the main characters, five to eight key events, the main themes, and two or three techniques. One page per text is enough.</li>
<li><strong>Collect quotations:</strong> aim for six to eight short ones per text.</li>
<li><strong>Write timed plans:</strong> take ten past questions and write only the five-line plans. This is quick and it trains selection.</li>
<li><strong>Write full answers weekly:</strong> one fully written essay per week, timed, and then mark it against the four-step PEEL pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Read model answers carefully:</strong> do not memorise them. Ask what the structure is, where the evidence is and how each paragraph links back to the question.</li>
</ol>
[[timeline Week 1::Summary sheets and quotation cards for each text ; Week 2::Five-line plans for twenty past questions ; Week 3::Two full timed essays per genre, marked by PEEL ; Week 4::Fix weak genre, revisit quotations, one more timed set]]
<p>Adjust the timeline to fit the weeks you actually have. The proportions matter more than the exact numbers: planning and writing take more of your time than rereading.</p>

<h2>What to do today</h2>
[[checklist Choose one past question and underline the command word and the topic ; Write a one-line answer to it ; Write one PEEL paragraph on it in ten minutes ; Make six quotation cards for one main character or theme ; Check the current WAEC syllabus and the list of prescribed texts ; Ask a classmate or teacher to mark your paragraph for point, evidence, explanation and link]]
`
