  content: `
<h2>Why a streak works when motivation does not</h2>
<p>Almost every student has made the same promise: "From Monday I will study every day." Monday arrives, motivation is high, and for three or four days all goes well. Then a bad night, a long day, a visitor or a tired afternoon breaks the pattern, and the whole promise collapses. Motivation is a feeling, and feelings change. A streak works because it replaces the feeling with a system.</p>
<p>A study streak is a simple chain of days. On each day you do a small, defined amount of study and mark it. The mark itself becomes a reward: you see the chain growing, and you do not want to break it. That visible record is the engine. The aim of this post is to show you how to design the chain so that it survives real life, because most streaks fail in the first fortnight due to design errors, not lack of character.</p>
<p>Nothing here guarantees any exam score. What a streak does is make sure you actually show up. Showing up daily, doing active work and reviewing mistakes is the foundation of nearly every good result.</p>
[[callout key|The rule that makes streaks survive|Make the daily requirement small enough that you could do it on the worst day of your month. A streak that needs a perfect day will end on the first imperfect one.]]

<h2>Step 1: Define a minimum that is too small to fail</h2>
<p>This is the most important design decision. Most people set a goal that suits their best day: two hours, ten pages, a full chapter. On a good day that is fine, but on a normal day it feels heavy, and a heavy task is exactly the thing we avoid.</p>
<p>Instead, define a <strong>minimum</strong>: the smallest study session that still counts. It should take between 20 and 30 minutes for most students and involve <em>active work</em>, not passive reading.</p>
<h3>What counts as study for your streak</h3>
<ul>
<li>Answering a set of past questions and checking the answers.</li>
<li>Writing a summary of a topic from memory, then checking it against your notes.</li>
<li>Solving a set of Mathematics or Physics problems and fixing the wrong ones.</li>
<li>Explaining a topic aloud to a friend, a sibling or an empty room.</li>
</ul>
<h3>What does not count</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sitting with a book open while your phone buzzes.</li>
<li>Highlighting a page without trying to recall it afterwards.</li>
<li>Watching a lesson video at double speed with no questions afterwards.</li>
</ul>
<p>Write your rule in one sentence and stick it on your wall. For example: "A day counts when I have answered at least ten questions and written down why I missed each wrong one." That sentence removes the daily argument about whether a session was enough.</p>
[[compare Fragile streak rule|Durable streak rule|Study for three hours every day ; Finish a whole chapter every night ; Only count perfect sessions ; Start when I feel ready|Ten questions and a short fix, every day ; One small topic a day, with questions ; Count the minimum, celebrate extra ; Start at a fixed cue, ready or not]]

<h2>Step 2: Attach it to a cue, not to a mood</h2>
<p>A habit needs a trigger: something that happens every day and tells your brain "now". Studying "when I have time" has no trigger, so it competes with everything else and usually loses. Choose a reliable cue that already exists in your day.</p>
<ul>
<li>Right after you bathe and dress in the morning.</li>
<li>Right after you return from school and eat.</li>
<li>Right after evening prayers or devotion.</li>
<li>Right after dinner, before you open your phone.</li>
</ul>
<p>The cue should be an event, not a time, because events move with you when your timetable shifts. If you wake up at different times on weekends, "after breakfast" still works where "7 p.m." might not.</p>
<h3>Prepare the place and the tools</h3>
<p>Remove friction. Keep your questions, notebook and pen on the study corner so that you do not spend ten minutes searching. If you use a phone app, open it once and leave it on the first question. If you study from a booklet, mark the page where you will start tomorrow before you close it today. A small preparation the night before can be the difference between starting and drifting.</p>
[[steps Choose the cue::Pick one daily event that always happens, such as after dinner. ; Choose the place::A fixed corner, table or bed edge, always the same. ; Prepare the tools::Booklet, pen, notebook and phone settings ready the night before. ; Start a two-minute timer::Agree with yourself to do just two minutes. Most days you will continue. ; Mark the day::Tick your calendar immediately after finishing, not later.]]

<h2>Step 3: Make progress visible</h2>
<p>The mark on the calendar is not decoration. It is feedback that tells your brain "this counts". Choose a tracker that you cannot avoid seeing.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A paper wall calendar:</strong> one square per day, a big tick or a coloured cross. Hang it where you will see it when you wake up.</li>
<li><strong>A hundred-box chart:</strong> draw ten rows of ten boxes in your notebook. Colour one box each day. At the end, you have a page of proof.</li>
<li><strong>A phone app or note:</strong> useful if you want reminders, but be careful that the phone does not distract you.</li>
</ul>
<p>Write the number on the calendar too: "Day 14". Numbers matter, because "day 14" is a small identity: I am someone who has studied fourteen days in a row.</p>
<h3>Milestones keep the middle from feeling empty</h3>
<p>The first week is exciting and the last week is near the finish line. The middle is where streaks die. Plan small rewards at fixed points: a favourite meal, an hour of football, a call with a friend, a new notebook. Rewards should be small, healthy and not related to your phone screen time.</p>
[[timeline Days 1 to 7::Prove it is possible. Minimum only, no extras. ; Days 8 to 21::Make it automatic. Protect the cue, keep the minimum small. ; Days 22 to 50::Add depth. Increase the minimum slightly or add a weekly timed set. ; Days 51 to 75::Add review. Use your error log and revisit old topics. ; Days 76 to 100::Finish strong. Add one full timed paper each week and reflect on your progress.]]

<h2>Step 4: Plan for the miss before it happens</h2>
<p>Every long streak will meet a bad day. You may be ill, travelling, grieving, trapped in a power cut or just exhausted. A good plan does not pretend these will not occur. It decides in advance what happens.</p>
<h3>The two-day rule</h3>
<p>Never miss two days in a row. One missed day is a blip. Two in a row is a pattern. After a miss, do the minimum the very next day, even if it is only five questions. This single rule saves more streaks than any app.</p>
<h3>The fallback minimum</h3>
<p>Create a smaller version of your daily task for terrible days, such as five questions or ten minutes of recall. On a day when you are ill or travelling, you do the fallback and the day still counts. The fallback is a rope bridge, not a lowered standard. Use it sparingly.</p>
<h3>Do not punish yourself</h3>
<p>If you miss a day, do not add the missed time to tomorrow. Do not label yourself lazy. Count the days again from where you are. The long-term score matters more than any single day. Think of it like a football season: no one expects to win every match, but you show up for the next one.</p>
[[qa A student studies on 94 of 100 days and misses 6 single days, never two in a row. Last month they studied 25 minutes on each of 27 days out of 30. How many minutes did they study last month, and what is their consistency for the 100 days?|Minutes last month: 27 days x 25 minutes = 675 minutes. ; Convert to hours: 675 divided by 60 = 11.25 hours. ; Consistency over 100 days: 94 studied days divided by 100 days = 94 percent. ; Because no two misses were adjacent, the two-day rule held, so the habit never broke for good.|675 minutes (11.25 hours) last month and 94 percent consistency. A real habit rarely needs 100 percent, but never missing twice in a row is what keeps it alive.]]

<h2>Step 5: Make the content of your study count</h2>
<p>A streak of empty sessions is just a streak of sitting down. To turn consistency into results, put quality into the minimum. You do not need to over-engineer this. Use a simple rotation that changes each day.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Day type</th><th>What you do in the minimum</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>New topic day</td><td>Read a short section, then answer ten questions on it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Review day</td><td>Redo questions you got wrong earlier and update your error log</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weak-spot day</td><td>Pick your weakest topic and attempt five hard questions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recall day</td><td>Write a one-page summary from memory, then compare with notes</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Rotate through these four across the week and add a longer timed practice on one weekend day, if your schedule allows. This gives your streak a shape: new learning, review and testing all get a turn. Over a hundred days, even a small daily dose produces a large body of work. Twenty-five minutes a day for 100 days is about 2,500 minutes, which is roughly 41 hours of focused study. That is a lot of questions answered and mistakes fixed.</p>
[[stats 100::days in the challenge ; 25::minutes a day as an example minimum ; 41::hours of focused study in total]]
<p>The 41 hours come from simple arithmetic: 25 x 100 = 2,500 minutes, and 2,500 divided by 60 is about 41.7. These figures are an example of how small amounts add up, not a statistic about any group of students.</p>

<h2>Ideas for your minimum, by subject</h2>
<p>Many students lose a week deciding what to do each day. Remove that decision by keeping a short menu for each subject. Pick one item from the menu for the day.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mathematics:</strong> solve five problems on one topic, then redo any you got wrong without looking at the solution. Write the formula you used beside each one.</li>
<li><strong>English:</strong> read one comprehension passage and answer its questions, or write five sentences using new words you met this week.</li>
<li><strong>Physics and Chemistry:</strong> recall a set of formulas, equations or definitions from memory, then check. Solve two calculation questions with units written out.</li>
<li><strong>Biology:</strong> draw and label one diagram from memory, then compare it with your textbook and correct it.</li>
<li><strong>Arts and social subjects:</strong> write a five-line answer to a past question from memory, then check the textbook for what you missed.</li>
<li><strong>Literature:</strong> write one point-evidence-explanation paragraph on a character or theme.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep this menu on the same page as your calendar. When the cue arrives, you pick and start. The less you have to think, the more often you will begin, and beginning is the hardest part of every session.</p>

<h2>Common reasons streaks fail and how to fix them</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The minimum is too big.</strong> Cut it in half. If you can say "I could do this when sick", it is the right size.</li>
<li><strong>There is no cue.</strong> If you cannot say when you will study, you will not. Pick the event.</li>
<li><strong>The tracker is hidden.</strong> If the calendar is inside a drawer, the streak is invisible. Put it where you will see it.</li>
<li><strong>The phone wins.</strong> Put it in another room for the minimum. Even better, keep it on do-not-disturb during the window.</li>
<li><strong>Perfectionism after a miss.</strong> "I broke it, so it is ruined." No. Restart the count, keep the old record as a personal best and carry on.</li>
<li><strong>Burnout.</strong> If you push long hours every day, you will crash. Keep one lighter day each week and sleep properly.</li>
<li><strong>Too much alone.</strong> Tell a friend or relative your streak. A weekly check-in makes it harder to quietly stop.</li>
</ul>
[[callout tip|Find a streak partner|Choose a classmate or friend and message each other a simple "done" after you study. Do not make it a competition about hours. The goal is accountability, not comparison.]]

<h2>What a bad week looks like and how to get through it</h2>
<p>Suppose you are on day 31 and a family event, a fever and a power cut arrive in the same week. Here is how the streak handles it. On the fever day you do the fallback: five questions on a topic you know well, or ten minutes of recall in bed. It counts. On the travel day you carry a small notebook and do three minutes of recall at the park and seven at your destination. It counts. On the day with the power cut you do the paper version: a written summary from memory by candlelight or phone torch. It counts. At the end of the week, your calendar has seven ticks and you have not studied more than an hour in total, yet the habit is intact.</p>
<p>This is the whole point of a small minimum. It turns the worst week of the month into a week of tiny wins instead of a week of failure. The students who reach day 100 are rarely the ones with the most free time. They are the ones who decided early that a very small effort on a very bad day is still a win.</p>
<h3>Review the streak every Sunday</h3>
<p>Spend five minutes each Sunday asking three questions. Did I keep the cue? What got in the way? What one small change would make next week easier? Write the answers in a line or two. If something keeps blocking you, such as a noisy evening or a tired afternoon, move the cue to a calmer part of the day. A streak is a system you improve, not a test you pass or fail.</p>

<h2>Using a streak around exams and school life</h2>
<p>A streak is not meant to fight your school timetable. It should fit around it. During term, your minimum might be done on the bus home, in the library or after dinner. During exam periods, you may increase the minimum or add a second block, but the streak itself should not become a source of stress.</p>
<h3>Match the streak to your exam</h3>
<p>If you are preparing for a particular exam such as WAEC, NECO, JAMB or university tests, make your daily minimum a piece of exam work: past questions, topic summaries or a weak-spot drill. Check the official body's website for the current syllabus and format so that you practise the right thing.</p>
<h3>Use the streak to build a bigger habit</h3>
<p>A hundred days is not magic. It is long enough that the habit becomes part of your day and short enough to feel reachable. When you finish, do not stop. Raise the minimum a little, set a new target, or begin a second habit such as reading a short article in English each day. A habit that has survived a hundred days is a strong foundation for the next goal.</p>

<h2>What to do today</h2>
[[checklist Write your one-sentence minimum rule and stick it on your wall ; Choose your daily cue and the place you will study ; Prepare your questions, pen and notebook for tomorrow ; Draw a 100-box chart or hang a calendar where you will see it ; Do your first minimum session today and tick Day 1 ; Tell one friend or relative about your streak]]
`
